  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Luigi d’Amato entered a Naples shipping dynasty whose maritime activity dated to 1870. He became an owner and president of Fratelli D’Amato, then took sole control of that family branch after separating it from his brothers’ businesses. Under his direction, the company renewed its fleet, remained a major carrier of raw materials and diversified from dry cargo into tankers and offshore-support vessels.\n\nThe prolonged shipping slump placed the group under mounting financial pressure. D’Amato pursued debt restructuring and liquidated several companies, but Fratelli D’Amato ultimately sold its last platform-supply vessels and exited shipping in 2018. His career nevertheless spanned the transformation of a traditional Neapolitan shipowning house into an international operator with offices in Europe and Asia and exposure across several maritime sectors.",
